>
  <p>"Study-life balance" sounds like a luxury you earn after the exams. It is the opposite: the rest is what makes the study work. A tired, resentful brain learns slowly, so running yourself into the ground is not dedication, it is inefficiency.</p>

  <h2 id="what">What balance really means</h2>
  <p>Balance is not splitting your day evenly between work and fun. It is keeping enough rest, sleep and enjoyment in your week that you can study with real focus when you sit down. The test is simple: can you keep this pace for a whole term without dreading it? If not, the plan is too heavy, however virtuous it feels.</p>

  <h2 id="rest">Rest that restores</h2>
  <p>Not all rest is equal. Scrolling between tasks leaves you more drained, not less. Real rest for students usually means three things backed by research: enough sleep, which Walker and Stickgold link directly to memory; daily movement, which Hillman shows improves attention and mood; and time doing something you find genuinely absorbing. Protect those three before you optimise anything else.</p>

  <h2 id="boundaries">Set boundaries</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A stop time.</strong> Pick an hour each night when study ends. Open-ended days expand to fill all your time and still feel unfinished.</li>
    <li><strong>Single-tasking.</strong> Study in <a href="/tools/pomodoro-timer/">focused blocks</a>, then stop cleanly. Half-studying all evening is the worst of both worlds.</li>
    <li><strong>Say no on purpose.</strong> You cannot do everything; choose what you drop rather than dropping sleep by default.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="plan">Plan room to live</h2>
  <p>If free time is "whatever is left", there will be none. Block your rest, exercise and social time into the <a href="/tools/timetable-maker/">timetable maker</a> first, then fit study around it with the <a href="/tools/study-plan-generator/">study plan generator</a>. Deci and Ryan's work shows motivation lasts longer when it comes from within, and nothing drains inner motivation faster than a life that is nothing but work. For the working-student version, see <a href="/guides/how-to-balance-study-and-work/">how to balance study and a part-time job</a>.</p>
</Guide>
